usage: minimal [-h] [--frames FRAMES] [--monitor MONITOR] [--bgra]
               [--source {auto,screen,wayland,window,synthetic,image}]
               [--capture-no-scale] [--size WxH] [--input-image PATH]
               [--param NAME=VALUE] [--work-scale F] [--hotkeys] [--indicator]
               [--no-indicator] [--ab-alternate SECONDS] [--report-gpu]
               [--report-diff] [--save-diff PATH] [--diff-gain DIFF_GAIN]
               [--windowed] [--prefetch] [--worker-env KEY=VALUE] [--bypass]
               [--send-ahead N] [--frame-timeout S] [--writev] [--shm]
               [--motion] [--no-motion] [--motion-guides] [--motion-small]
               [--worker-log] [--headless] [--save-before PATH]
               [--save-after PATH] [--list-monitors]

NeuralScreen MVP — capture, DLSS5 neural pass, display.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --frames FRAMES       stop after N frames (0 = until the window is closed)
  --monitor MONITOR     screen index (default 0)
  --bgra                hand the synthetic card over in BGRA order, the way a
                        capture card does, so the handoff can be timed without
                        a screen
  --source {auto,screen,wayland,window,synthetic,image}
                        where frames come from: auto (portal on Wayland, X11
                        grab otherwise), screen, wayland, window (the portal
                        asks for ONE window instead of a screen — cheaper, and
                        what a game stream wants), a generated test card, or a
                        still image
  --capture-no-scale    drop videoscale from the capture pipeline. The chain
                        measures 33% of a core at 2560x1440 — about 42ms of
                        CPU per frame — and this removes one 14.7MB pass from
                        it. The risk is caps negotiation failing when the
                        portal's reported size and the stream's disagree
                        (fractional scaling), which is loud rather than silent
  --size WxH            force the frame size for sources that can make one
                        (synthetic). Lets the 2560x1440 matrix be measured
                        without the portal and so without a dialog — which is
                        how the 1440p cost gets decomposed instead of guessed.
                        Required by --source window: a window stream reports
                        no size, and frames are scaled to this
  --input-image PATH    the frame to replay with --source image
  --param NAME=VALUE    override an NR parameter; repeatable. Known:
                        auto_mask, intensity, local_structure, local_tone,
                        preset, profile, skin_structure, style, ui_correction.
                        e.g. --param intensity=0 to dial the effect off
  --work-scale F        fraction of the frame the network works on, 0<F<=1
                        (default 1.0). The result is scaled back to full size,
                        so this buys frame rate without shrinking the output:
                        the cost falls with the square of the scale. Lower it
                        if the pass is too slow, at the cost of the effect's
                        own resolution
  --hotkeys             register global hotkeys: Ctrl+Alt+1 toggles the
                        enhancement, Ctrl+Alt+Q quits. Global, so they work
                        while the game window has focus; needs a running X
                        server (XWayland is enough)
  --indicator           draw an ON/OFF badge in the top-left corner of the
                        output window (default: on unless --headless). It is
                        drawn by the renderer, not into the frame, so a saved
                        PNG stays clean
  --no-indicator        do not draw the badge
  --ab-alternate SECONDS
                        flip the enhancement on and off every SECONDS. An A/B
                        with no keypress, which matters because a global X
                        grab does not see keys pressed in a Wayland-native
                        window
  --report-gpu          sample GPU utilisation for the life of the run and
                        report it with the timings. A frame that is slow while
                        the GPU is idle is one better code could fix; one that
                        is slow at 98% means the work has to come out
  --report-diff         print how far the pass moved the picture: per-channel
                        MAD, maximum, PSNR and the share of pixels that
                        changed by more than 2 and 8 levels
  --save-diff PATH      write the difference as a PNG, amplified so a one-
                        level change is visible (see --diff-gain)
  --diff-gain DIFF_GAIN
                        amplification for --save-diff (default 8: 128 is no
                        change, +/-8 levels maps to the full range at gain 16)
  --windowed            draw in a window instead of fullscreen
  --prefetch            drain the frame source on its own thread and always
                        process the newest frame. A screencast whose client
                        stops consuming stops producing, so without this the
                        capture leg absorbs a producer round trip every frame
                        (measured: 238ms per grab in the loop against 13ms
                        with nothing else running). Costs a busy core
  --worker-env KEY=VALUE
                        set an environment variable in the worker process
                        only. Repeatable. The policy's [entry.env] applies to
                        every run of that entry, so this is how one run turns
                        on a port-side switch (DLSS5VK_SERVE_SPLIT=1, a memory
                        setting) without editing the policy or affecting other
                        runs
  --bypass              NR OFF: the worker skips NGX entirely and hands back
                        the frame it was given. A truer control than --param
                        intensity=0, which may still run the network at zero
                        strength: this one separates the network from the
                        texture upload/readback when hunting the per-frame
                        cost
  --send-ahead N        keep N frames in flight instead of sending one and
                        waiting for it (default 1 = the original serial loop).
                        The worker fed with no client at all runs 42.5fps at
                        1440p against the pipeline's 9.1, and the
                        serialisation is most of that difference
  --frame-timeout S     how long to wait for a reply before calling it a stall
                        (default 60s). A stall stops the run and names itself
                        rather than hanging: the worker answers in order, so
                        after a missing reply every later reply is
                        unattributable
  --writev              hand each frame to the kernel in one scatter-gather
                        write instead of four separate ones. Each separate
                        write can block on a full pipe while the worker polls
                        with a Sleep(8), and the loop pays a fixed ~45ms a
                        frame in send at every frame size — while the worker's
                        own timestamps say it delivers a 64KB frame in 0.21ms
  --shm                 put the frame in a shared section for the worker to
                        read, and take the result pixels from another: 14.7MB
                        each way at 2560x1440 that otherwise go down a pipe.
                        The native worker implements both; the Wine worker
                        refuses and frames stay on the pipe
  --motion              send the motion field after all. Not a quality
                        decision - the field is all zeros and the native
                        worker's no-field path is byte-identical - but the
                        Wine worker derives flow from it, so the NGX arm needs
                        this on. Off by default: 14.7MB and ~5ms a frame at
                        2560x1440 for bytes that change no pixel
  --no-motion           do not send the motion field (the default; kept so
                        existing run requests and scripts still parse)
  --motion-guides       estimate the motion field with guides.py (DIS optical
                        flow on a downscaled gray frame, noise floor applied)
                        instead of sending zeros. Implies the field goes out,
                        so the default no-motion path is off for that run.
                        Needs OpenCV (cv2) on this machine.
  --motion-small        send the motion field at the optical-flow size
                        (320x180) and let the worker upscale it. NOT USABLE in
                        the pipe path: measured on the box, the worker goes
                        silent for 60s and never answers frame 0. Kept for the
                        wire format and the tests; see docs/M0-FINDINGS.md
  --worker-log          echo the worker's stderr as it arrives — its warm-up
                        and its per-frame timings — instead of only printing
                        it when a run fails, which is why a slow first frame
                        was invisible
  --headless            run without presenting (for tests and CI)
  --save-before PATH    write the frame the pass was given, from the same
                        iteration as --save-after, as a PNG
  --save-after PATH     write the last processed frame as a PNG
  --list-monitors       print the screens and exit
